 "substitutedBy": null,
 "summary": "Requires the collection of certain demographic information by certain state agencies, boards and commissions.",
 "title": "Requires the collection of certain demographic information by certain state agencies, boards and commissions",
 "vetoMessages": {
  "items": [
   {
    "billId": {
     "basePrintNo": "A677",
     "basePrintNoStr": "A677-2019",
     "printNo": "A677",
     "session": 2019,
     "version": ""
    },
    "billPage": 0,
    "chapter": 0,
    "lineEnd": 0,
    "lineStart": 0,
    "memoText": "\n \n                         VETO MESSAGE - No. 172\n \nTO THE ASSEMBLY:\n \nI am returning herewith, without my approval, the following bill:\n \nAssembly Bill Number 677, entitled:\n \n    \"AN  ACT  to  amend  the Executive Law, in relation to requiring the\n      collection of certain demographic  information  by  certain  state\n      agencies, boards and commissions\"\n \n    NOT APPROVED\n \n  I  applaud  the  sponsors  for  their  efforts to address the critical\nissues presented by this  legislation.  Unquestionably,  New  York  must\ncontinue  to  work to improve its data collection systems and practices.\nHowever, while laudable in purpose, this bill would create new financial\nobligations and operational intricacies not  accounted  for  within  the\nState's  financial  plan, and with insufficient time for implementation.\nSuch funding decisions should be addressed within  the  context  of  the\nbudget.\n \n  New York is currently working to upgrade its data collection system in\nways  that  are both culturally sensitive and technologically adept. New\nYorkers deserve accurate public information  and  public  records  about\nevery  community  across  New  York State. This includes Asian American,\nNative Hawaiian, and Pacific Islander populations.\n \n  While I am vetoing this bill, I am now directing the Governor's Execu-\ntive Committee for Diversity, Inclusion, and Equal Opportunity to  begin\nwork  with  state agencies to better understand the issues, concerns and\nbarriers to enhanced demographic data  collection  for  Asian  American,\nNative  Hawaiian,  Pacific  Islander,  and other racial and ethnic popu-\nlations in New York State. This will involve working to  determine  best\npractices  for  the  collection  of  racial  and ethnic data, including,\nprimary language, place of birth,  and  country  of  origin  across  all\nracial  and ethnic subgroups, so that such questions allow us to collect\ndata without alarming New Yorkers as to fears of misuse of this data. in\nparticular, given the overly aggressive approach the federal  government\nhas  taken  to  immigration  enforcement  I am concerned that the bill's\nrequirement to collect data regarding an individual's place of birth and\nnational origin will have unintended consequences that could  be  disas-\ntrous for New Americans.\n \n  Additionally,  I  am directing state agencies who already collect this\ntype of demographic data to take steps to ensure that such data is  made\navailable to the public and is included in any relevant public reporting\nmechanisms  utilized  by  those  agencies  while safeguarding privacy of\nindividuals. State agencies will work with the Executive  Committee  for\nDiversity,  Inclusion  and  Equal Opportunity to consider both the costs\nand operational impact of collecting more accurate  data  to  accomplish\nthe  underlying  goals  of  this  legislation.  While  this bill must be\nvetoed, I am committed to working to ensure its  purpose  is  ultimately\nfulfilled.\n \n  This bill is disapproved.                   (signed) ANDREW M. CUOMO\n \n                               __________\n",
    "signedDate": null,
    "signer": "ANDREW M. CUOMO",
